  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Re-verify all four premises above at HEAD before editing — they were wrong once and the point of this rescope is not to trust a note. Confirm the caller count with ripgrep, not `grep -rn` (which times out on this tree).</step>
    <step order="1">Correct the `EntityCreationService.ts` comment: two callers not three, both classified parentless by 076, and DROP the "is task 093, after which this method should lose callers" clause — that sentence is what makes a reader think the method is on its way out. Keep the genuinely load-bearing warning that this route stores bytes in the caller's BU container and is not reversible for a secure record.</step>
    <step order="2">Correct the two call-site comments (`CreateAnalysisWizardWidget.tsx`, `createXrmEmailComposeHandlers.ts`) the same way. ONLY these two — see the collision constraint.</step>
    <step order="3">Add the ordering regression test and perturb it to prove it bites.</step>
    <step order="4">Re-home the residue, in writing: (a) the live "files land in the project's own container" assertion belongs to task **047**, which has the environment to run it; (b) secure **MATTER** provisioning is owner-deferred (`provision-project` is project-only) and needs its own task if the owner wants it. Record both in notes/task-093-close-out.md rather than leaving them implicit in a closed task.</step>
    <step order="5">Write notes/task-093-close-out.md: the four false premises with their evidence, the classification resolution + its owner flag, and the two re-homings.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If the ordering regression test cannot be made to fail when the ordering is broken — e.g. because the type system already makes the broken form unrepresentable — say so and pin the contract at the type level instead (a compile-fail fixture or a signature assertion). Do NOT ship a test that passes both before and after the break; that is the decorative-test failure mode this project keeps finding.</trigger>
    <trigger>If any of the four premises turns out to be TRUE at HEAD after all — in particular if a second `SecureProjectSection.tsx` exists, or `uploadFilesWithoutRecord` has a third caller — STOP and report. That would mean the rescope itself was built on a stale reading, and the original scope may still be live.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`EntityCreationService` already enforces create-first by signature; task 076 already performed the cutover; the single `SecureProjectSection.tsx` already renders the secure choice.</existing>
    <extension>Nothing new is built. This retires stale prose and adds one regression pin to an existing contract.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Three comments in shipped code tell every future reader that a reorder is still pending and assigned to this task. One of them also says a live, deliberate API "should lose callers", which invites someone to delete a route that 076 classified as correct. The stale comment outliving its mechanism is this project's single most repeated defect — 19 instances and counting.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

